## What I'd change and why

I implemented it exactly as asked: for each new line in the loop, when its Discount is blank I call `PricingRules.standardDiscountFor(line.Product2Id)` and assign the result. Lines that already carry a Discount — including an explicit 0 — are left untouched, because the guard is `Discount == null`. The discount rules stay entirely in PricingRules; nothing is copied into the handler.

One thing I'd flag to the pricing team (without editing their class): `standardDiscountFor` runs a SOQL query, so calling it per line means one query per line. A quote can carry up to 200 lines in a single insert, which would exceed the 100 SOQL-queries-per-transaction limit. The clean fix, without changing the rules or any call site, is for them to add a small static Id-keyed cache inside `standardDiscountFor`. Callers would still write `standardDiscountFor(line.Product2Id)`, the rules stay in one place, and every caller becomes bulk-safe.

