     * @return one contact Id per lead (same order as {@code leads}), or {@code null} where no contact matches
     */
    public static List<Id> matchByEmail(List<Lead> leads, List<Contact> contacts) {
        List<Id> matchedContactIds = new List<Id>();
        if (leads == null) {
            return matchedContactIds;
        }

        Map<String, Id> contactEmailToId = buildFirstContactByEmail(contacts);

        for (Lead lead : leads) {
            matchedContactIds.add(matchLeadEmail(lead, contactEmailToId));
        }

        return matchedContactIds;
    }

    /**
     * Builds a map from normalized email to the Id of the <em>first</em> contact (in
     * {@code contacts} order) that has that email. Blank emails are skipped, and a key is
     * only ever mapped once so that ties resolve to the earliest contact in the list.
     */
    private static Map<String, Id> buildFirstContactByEmail(List<Contact> contacts) {
        Map<String, Id> contactEmailToId = new Map<String, Id>();
        if (contacts == null) {
            return contactEmailToId;
        }

        for (Contact contact : contacts) {
            String normalizedEmail = toNormalizedEmail(contact.Email);
            if (normalizedEmail == null) {
                continue;
            }
            if (!contactEmailToId.containsKey(normalizedEmail)) {
                contactEmailToId.put(normalizedEmail, contact.Id);
            }
        }

        return contactEmailToId;
    }

    /**
     * Looks up the contact Id whose normalized email equals the lead's normalized email,
     * or {@code null} when there is no match or the lead email is blank.
     */
    private static Id matchLeadEmail(Lead lead, Map<String, Id> contactEmailToId) {
        String leadEmail = toNormalizedEmail(lead.Email);
        if (leadEmail == null) {
            return null;
        }
        return contactEmailToId.get(leadEmail);
    }

    /**
     * Normalizes an email for comparison: trims surrounding whitespace and lower-cases it.
     * Returns {@code null} for a null, empty or whitespace-only value so that blank emails
     * never match on either side.
     */
    private static String toNormalizedEmail(String email) {
        if (email == null) {
            return null;
        }
        String normalized = email.trim().toLowerCase();
        return normalized.length() == 0 ? null : normalized;
    }
}
```

## Why it failed and the fix

The test `importOf3000` runs 3,000 leads against 3,000 contacts. The nested-loop version was O(leads × contacts) ≈ 9M iterations, each re-normalizing the contact email — that exceeded the Apex CPU time limit.

I changed the approach to O(leads + contacts):

1. **Build a map once** — iterate the contacts a single time, normalizing each contact email exactly once, and store the **first** contact Id per normalized email. Using `containsKey` before `put` preserves the "first contact in `contacts` order wins" tie-break.
2. **Look up per lead** — normalize each lead email once and do a single O(1) `Map.get`.

This keeps every requirement intact: blank/null/whitespace emails never match, comparison is trimmed and case-insensitive, ties resolve to the earliest contact in list order, the result matches `leads` in size and order, and there is still no query or DML.

## What the error actually means

The key detail is the word **`void`** in the message:

```
Method does not exist or incorrect signature: void sendEmail(Messaging.SingleEmailMessage) from the type Messaging
```

It only fired on line 63 — the line `if (Messaging.sendEmail(email))` — and nowhere else (my `Messaging.SingleEmailMessage` usage compiled fine). That tells me the method **does** resolve, but the version visible in this org returns **`void`**, not `Boolean`. Using a `void` call as the condition of an `if` is an "incorrect signature", which is exactly the error reported.

So the fix is to **call `Messaging.sendEmail(email)` as a statement, not as a Boolean expression**. Calling a method as a bare statement is valid whether it returns a `Boolean` or nothing, and it still satisfies the requirement: one send per opportunity, inside the loop, with the result of each send isolated so a failure doesn't stop the rest. A successful send is treated as "no exception thrown" and counted.

